Nearly 60 Cambodian troops complete UN civilian defense training and the Australian Government-sponsored Gender Peace and Peace Forum
(Phnom Penh): The United Nations (UN) Civil Defense Training and the Gender Forum for Peace and Peace, the second sponsorship of the Australian Government, have successfully concluded. This training aims to provide knowledge, skills and laws on the protection of civilians, women and children, which is one of the seven priorities of the action for peace, in addition to the core tasks of the peacekeeping force.
The Closing Ceremony was held in the presence of General Sem Sovanny, First Vice President of the National Committee and Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, and Justin Whyatt, Ambassador of Australia to Cambodia, and Major General Ruth EISLEY, Commander of the Australian College for War research at the National Peacekeeping Center this morning, October 27, 2023.
The course lasts for 4 days from 23 to 26 October 2023, with a total of 57 participants, 37 women, with intensive training from the Australian military.
General Sem Sovanny expressed his deep gratitude to the Australian Government for its continuous training of Cambodian Blue Hat forces, especially the Australian military, who shared their experiences and important lessons to broaden their understanding.
The General added that the Gender Forum for Peace and Peace is an important topic to promote the "Women's Agenda for Peace and Security" as the capacity of women in uniform has been contributing to improving efficiency and effectiveness. Stability in the mission area. This course is another source of pride for our motherland and a beacon for Cambodian participants by bringing better knowledge and skills.
He added that Cambodia-Australia has a strong relationship for a long time and we want to be a true and loyal partner for further cooperation. Australia is pleased that Cambodia has increased its role in the UN peacekeeping operation.
Lt. Gen. Ruth EISLEY was very proud to see more Blue Hat women in the peacekeeping operation, as she was the only woman in the first peacekeeping mission. She hopes that all female soldiers will be an important part of the peacekeeping operation.
General Sem Sovanny is highly committed to continuing to prioritize peacekeeping operations and strengthen peace and stability in the country, as well as to continue humanitarian activities through participation in UN missions, which is in line with the words of Antonio Guterres. (Antonio Guterres) UN Secretary-General, emphasizing global peacekeeping and security operations.
