Royal Government vows to improve climate-resilient livelihoods for rice-growing communities in the Tonle Sap region
The purpose of this workshop is to provide an overview of the project to stakeholders, receive feedback and discuss actions and strategies to implement to achieve the planned results.
Dr. Yang Saing Koma, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that the Royal Government of Cambodia has identified specific needs to help smallholder farmers to improve their livelihoods, national food security, economy and climate-resilient farming systems.
He said the government has set two priority policy programs for the agricultural sector through the introduction of coordination mechanisms and financing programs that promote agricultural production, market access and price stability at reasonable levels, as well as the deployment of agricultural technical officers. All communes with agricultural activities throughout the country and establish rural farming communities.
He said that the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries is paying close attention to reducing poverty through the development of agriculture, which is a priority sector that has contributed significantly to national economic development and is a major source of Livelihoods for the majority of rural people.
The Ministry of Environment, through the General Department of Local Communities, has set strategic priorities for the development of local communities, focusing on the creation and diversification of occupations, which are an important part of contributing to improving local livelihoods and increasing income. Agriculture, eco-tourism services and other occupations that do not affect the natural resources in the region.
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment Mom Thany said that the Tonle Sap Basin is not only a potential geographical area but also a lifeline for millions of Cambodians who depend on resources for their daily livelihoods.
She added that the General Department of Local Communities will implement the PCRL project in collaboration with relevant institutions, the private sector and development partners to improve the livelihoods of communities around the Tonle Sap region, natural resource management and biodiversity conservation, especially to ensure Sustainable development in terms of environment, economy, society, culture and politics.
Dr. Chou Chey Thirith, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Agriculture and Chairman of the PCRL Project, said that the project aims to reduce the climate vulnerability of the rice growing community and increase the resilience of the growing community to climate change. Rice in the Tonle Sap region through the implementation of ecological production methods based on ecosystems and market dynamics.
PCRL projects are implemented in five target provinces around Tonle Sap Lake: Pursat, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey, Siem Reap and Kampong Thom, which are important rice growing areas with heavy rainfall, but limited access to irrigation and vulnerable communities. Most have little adaptation to climate change.
