(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian side received a grant from the Japanese side totaling approximately $ 9.7 million to implement the "project to build a permanent satellite station network across the country" in order to accelerate cadastral surveying and strengthen relevant administrative services. And land registration.
The cooperation was carried out through an exchange of letters and related documents on the grant funding of the Government of Japan, while the relevant documents were signed by Mr. Sok Chenda Sophea, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation with Mr. Ek Ambassador UENO Atsushi on October 26, 2023.
The grant from the Government of Japan to the Royal Government of Cambodia is 1.338 million yen (one hundred and thirty-three million yen) or about 9.7 million US dollars (nine click seven million dollars). United States) to implement a project to build a network of permanent satellite stations across the country.
The project aims to accelerate cadastral surveys and strengthen administrative services related to land registration through the installation of permanent satellite stations to contribute to the promotion of infrastructure projects.
"As Cambodia develops its infrastructure and land as rapidly as it does now, the installation of permanent satellite stations across the country is very important," both sides said.
