The Drum Museum is not only a museum that collects and studies the culture of ethnic minorities, it is also the only place where copper drums represent the most powerful in the world.
In celebration of the 50th anniversary of the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region in 2008, the Chinese government approved the construction of a large Chinese ethnic museum in central Nanning, Guangxi, to open to national and international tourists. Free and get to know the culture and civilization of the minority related to the use of drums represented at that time.
The Giant Drum Museum is located on the banks of the Yangtze River south of Qingshu Mountain, covers an area of more than 8,667 square meters and has four floors, and was officially opened in 2009. The main purpose of this giant drum museum is to gather, study, preserve and promote the culture of 12 ethnic groups living in China, Southeast Asia.
The museum exhibits ancient copper drums, some dating from the first century CE to the fourth and seventh centuries AD. Drums are considered by the Chinese people to be important representations of warlords and leaders used to bring success. The drums in the gallery have a variety of designs and meanings, with the bells carving frogs on the drum, a symbol of the frog's gentleness and good fortune, the cool rain.
This tourist street in the center of Nanning is very attractive for local tourists to visit. This tourist site has a history associated with the reigns of many Chinese emperors, including the Song Dynasty, the Qing Dynasty, and the Han Dynasty.
This tourist street attracts not only young people but also the elderly to visit this area.
