The October 23, 1991 peace agreement was widely debated, with some claiming that it was due to the decline of Soviet influence and the dissolution of the Cold War, as well as the country's withdrawal. As a reference to the Cambodian side of the conflict, etc., and some politicians also try to explain in various ways to gain political benefits from October 23.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the founder of the peace talks, once stressed that peace talks could not have taken place without Samdech Norodom Sihanouk and his statue. Samdech Techo confirmed that both characters are the most important characters.
Through the message of Samdech Techo Hun Sen mentioned above, it is clear that the talks of October 23, 1991 were born out of the spirit of national unity, born of Khmer and Khmer.
Although the Paris Peace Accords did not completely end the war as the Khmer Rouge continued to take refuge in the jungles of the border, it also helped pave the way for Samdech Techo Hun Sen to pursue a win-win policy. The Khmer Rouge out of the jungle brought an end to the war from the end of 1998 to the present.
Although the provisions of the Paris Peace Agreement of October 23, 1991 have been incorporated into the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia on September 24, 1994, the agreement clearly demonstrates its progress towards completion. Conflict between Khmer and Khmer Krom, negotiated as a Khmer family, not foreign.
The legacy of October 23, 1991 has become a mirror of the spirit of unity that Cambodian politicians of all persuasions and all Cambodians should adhere to in resolving national issues to avoid the flames of war and push the nation forward.
Not only that, October 23, 1991 is also an event to remind the Cambodian people of the suffering of the Cambodian people because of the flames of war and the importance of peace, which must be jointly protected and maintained, not only in your extremist ideas and atrocities. Extremist politics that leads to national catastrophe. By Van Saray Australia may reach agreement with China over Australian wine tax dispute
The Prime Minister of Australia will pay a state visit to China in early November to meet with President Xi Jinping as the two trading partners work out a settlement of the dispute between the two countries, the Canberra government said on Sunday. Two re.
"I look forward to the visit to China, which is an important step towards ensuring a stable and productive relationship," Albanese said in a statement. "I welcome the progress we have made in bringing Australian products, including Australian wines, back to the Chinese market," he said.
The much-anticipated visit will be the first visit to China by an Australian prime minister since 2016.
China has raised tariffs on key Australian exports such as wheat, beef and wine by 2020, sparking a bitter dispute with the Asian economic power with Australia's former conservative government.
China has also stopped imports of some of Australia's key commodities, including coal, blocking multibillion-dollar trade flows.
This year, China dropped tariffs on wheat, lifted a ban on timber imports and agreed to resume Australian coal tariffs.
