LONDON (Reuters) - Crude prices fell about 1 percent on Thursday as the United States eased sanctions on Venezuela to allow more oil to flow back into world markets, but Israel's military campaign in the Gaza Strip remains. Concerns of the oil market.
The United States has issued a six-month license to reopen OPEC member Venezuela's energy sector after reaching an agreement between the Venezuelan government and the opposition to advance elections this year. 2024 is fair.
Experts say the deal is not expected to quickly expand Venezuela's oil output, but could boost profits by helping some foreign companies return to production and supply oil. The country's crude reaches a wider range of customers. According to Reuters.
IMF, Bangladesh agree on first phase review of $ 4.7 billion bailout
Bangladesh's $ 416 billion economy has been one of the fastest growing economies in the world for many years, but recently the country has struggled to pay for fuel imports as capital has risen. Its dollar-denominated foreign exchange reserves fell by more than a third as imports became more expensive after Russia's invasion of Ukraine.
Reuters reports, citing an IMF statement, that the completion of the first phase of the review, which is still pending approval by the IMF board in the coming weeks, will release about 681 loans. Million dollars for this country.
"The authorities have made significant progress on structural reforms under the IMF-supported program, but challenges remain," the IMF said. "Continued global financial tightening, along with existing vulnerabilities, is putting macroeconomic controls at risk, putting pressure on the taka and foreign exchange reserves," the IMF added.
The IMF approved a $ 4.7 billion loan to Bangladesh in January with an immediate disbursement of about $ 476 million, making it the first country in the world to receive such funding. Three South Asian countries applied for bailouts last year amid economic turmoil.
The country of 170 million people is battling chronic high inflation, fueled by rising energy and food prices, along with a devaluation of the currency that has given Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina's government a headache. .
The IMF forecasts that Bangladesh's economic growth will reach 6 percent in fiscal 2024, while inflation is projected to average above 7 percent by the end of the year. According to Reuters.
