Cambodia Closes to Host 3rd Conference on Mine Victim and Explosive Ordnance Disposal
(Phnom Penh): After a three-day event on the evening of October 19, 2023, Cambodia held a closing ceremony to host the 3rd Global Conference on Mine Victim and Remnants. Explosives from the war in the context of disability rights.
The Closing Ceremony of the 3rd Global Conference was presided over by Dr. Neth Savoeun, Acting Prime Minister, on behalf of His Excellency Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and was attended by Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Vice President, Min Authority, Prince. Mirrett Seth al-Hussein, envoy of the Convention against the Use of Anti-Personnel Mines, as well as several other stakeholders.
He said that the global conference provided many opportunities to discuss, share and exchange experiences to reflect, motivate, learn and improve in victim assistance, use of assistive technology, use of data, mental health support, rehabilitation services. Health care, social equity and enhanced cooperation, as well as increased understanding of the complexities of the problems and opportunities in the relief work.
In the past, the Royal Government of Cambodia has actively worked to support victims and people with disabilities through the implementation of policies, national strategic plans to improve livelihoods, growth and employment opportunities, education and protection of human rights. Disability as well as technical standards, physical infrastructure to create conditions for people with disabilities.
Acting Prime Minister Neth Savoeun affirmed that the Royal Government of Cambodia has launched a National Strategic Plan on Disability 2018-2023, with careful consideration of addressing economic, employment and employment opportunities for people with disabilities.
At the same time, the Royal Government has issued a sub-decree on setting rates and procedures for the recruitment of persons with disabilities, including 2% for the public sector and 1% for the private sector. 3,748 (23% female) work in 37 ministries, institutions and 5,839 private employees with disabilities (66% female) work in 291 private institutions.
In addition, the rights of persons with disabilities are promoted in society, along with political life, social activities, culture, sports and activities that demonstrate non-discrimination against persons with disabilities in Cambodia. This is according to what the Acting Prime Minister Neth Savoeun emphasized.
