Please be informed that the decision to close the "Metro AV" polyclinic came after 18 Cambodians with kidney problems, commonly known as kidneys, have been lying in wait for the kidney transplant service in India for months. And did not receive the service because of the money to pay for the treatment, the representative of the Metro Polyclinic escaped.
In order to receive kidney transplant services in India, a patient from Banteay Meanchey province, who asked to remain anonymous, paid about US $ 50,000 to Nget Sam Oun, director general of the Metro LV Polyclinic in Phnom Penh.
The patient went on to say that after receiving the money, he was sent to India for medical treatment. After arriving in India, he did not receive the kidney transplant service as promised by Metro Polyclinic.
He has been waiting for a kidney transplant in India for seven months. According to the patient, he did not receive the kidney transplant service because Nget Sam Oun, the director general of the Metro Polyclinic in Phnom Penh, did not pay to go to the hospital. In India. When asked by representatives of the Metro Polyclinic in Phnom Penh, they claimed that the company in Phnom Penh went bankrupt and had no money to pay for the treatment.
In addition to the patients who provided the above information, there are 17 other Cambodian patients who are waiting for a solution and receiving kidney transplant services. But the wait dragged on for months without a solution, and Nget Sam Oun, the recipient of the money, escaped.
While the light of access to kidney transplants does not know the results, they also have to pay for dialysis and more food.
These 18 Cambodian people with kidney disease and looking forward to the opportunity to continue breathing through kidney transplantation have called for the intervention of Samdech Hun Sen, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, as they have no hope for Mr. Nget Sam Oun Akka The director of the Metro Polyclinic in Phnom Penh will not come forward to solve that.
