The International Monetary Fund (IMF) said last weekend that China's weak economic recovery and the risk of a protracted real estate crisis could hurt its economic outlook. More Asia. According to Reuters.
The IMF says China's post-closure stimulus measures have lost momentum faster than expected.
Reuters reports that the IMF said in a blog post on Asia's economic outlook that the strength of the US economy has provided less support for Asia than ever before as the superpower focuses on the services sector, Reuters reported. Which does not stimulate demand for exports.
The IMF said: "In the short term, strong reforms in China's heavily indebted real estate sector and the slowdown in economic activity resulting from the reforms are likely to spread in the region. "Especially for exporters with close trade ties with China."
"On the downside, the real estate crisis is prolonging and the limited policy response in China will exacerbate the region's economic downturn," the IMF said.
In its report on the global economic outlook released during the IMF annual meeting in Marrakesh, Morocco last week, the IMF dropped its 2024 growth forecast for Asia. The 4.4 percent forecast in April was 4.2 percent, down from the 4.6 percent forecast for this year. According to Reuters.
