The provincial governor also requested all relevant authorities to monitor all concerns in the local area and participate in solving them in a timely, effective and efficient manner. Mine Authority: Mines and Explosive Ordnance Disaster Relief Works Not Just for Humanitarian Work but for Human Rights and Public Health
(Phnom Penh): On behalf of the Prime Minister Hun Manet, Dr. Ly Thuch, Senior Minister and First Vice President of the Mines Authority, said that the relief of mine victims and explosive remnants is not just a humanitarian work. It is a human rights and public health work.
He was speaking at the opening of the Third World Conference on the Relief of Landmine and Explosive Ordnance Disposal in the Context of Disability Rights, co-organized by the Mine Action Authority and the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women. Use of anti-personnel mines (Ottawa Convention).
The Global Conference was held on October 17, 2023 at the Sofitel Phuket Hotel, with the aim of providing input for the Ottawa Convention Action Plan and providing a global forum for communities and victims of landmines and national remnants. Explosions express their concerns and problems, including sharing experiences, knowledge, technology and new ideas in rescuing mine victims and explosive remnants.
The Global Conference brings together about 200 victims, experts in the field of disaster relief, human rights, human rights, health, development and other sectors from 45 member countries and non-members of the Ottawa Convention.
The Senior Minister highlighted the importance of mine action relief and the need to integrate disaster relief into national policies and plans as well as legal frameworks to ensure that all victims have access to land. Full rights and support. The Victim Relief Coalition also reflects the obligations of the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities and the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals.
He believes that this conference is not only a forum for sharing experiences and knowledge, but it is also a time for expressing the spirit of unity in fulfilling the global victims' obligations.
The workshop was chaired by Prof. Ngy Meng, Director of the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital and Director of the National Eye Health Program, Dr. Kros Sarath, Siem Reap Provincial Health Department, and Roshan Bista, Coordinating Officer of the Cambodia Service Fund. Of professors, doctors and eye health experts, as well as about 30 relevant partners from provinces such as Takeo, Kampot, Pursat, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey and Siem Reap.
Since 1999, the Service Foundation has partnered with the Ministry of Health of Cambodia through the National Eye Health Program to support the training of eye doctors and nurses in providing eye care and treatment services to Cambodians.
He said that the vision program of the service fund has been implemented in Siem Reap since 1999, which has supported the Siem Reap Regional Eye Hospital and Angkor Hospital for Children on the development of existing vision by providing support on Training for ophthalmologists, optometrists and optometrists both inside and outside the country.
At the same time, Prof. Ngy Meng emphasized in the workshop program that the eye health sector is growing steadily in Cambodia, we achieved a blindness prevalence rate from 0.38% in 2007 to 0.37. % In 2019 and we are committed to reducing this valuation rate to 0.35% by 2030.
The aim is to improve and enhance the quality of compliance with the standards to ensure the provision of eye care services, time, quality, safety, efficiency and reasonable prices at all levels of eye care services.
He added that today's workshop is a contribution and very important for the six provinces that are partners of service organizations, and it is one of the inputs of the minimum standard of eye care that Cambodia is preparing. He strongly hopes that today's workshop will achieve its goals as planned.
