NEW YORK (Reuters) - A group of Sri Lankan private creditors holding eurozone debt has proposed a debt restructuring plan to the government by issuing 10 bonds linked to the country's aging macroeconomics. Between 2027 and 2023. According to Reuters.
According to the proposal, these creditors who choose bonds attached to macroeconomics will reduce their principal by 20 percent.
Sri Lanka's private creditors have submitted a new proposal on how to restructure $ 12 billion in foreign debt, Reuters reported last Thursday.
The proposal indicates that the coupon rate or interest rate will be paid in cash and non-cash, and the cash coupon rate from 2028 will be between 8% and 9.5% depending on the maturity period.
