According to a report read by Liv Sovanna, Secretary-General of the Bar Association, the provision of legal aid to the poor under the auspices of the Royal Government through the Ministry of Justice focuses on two activities: protection of the poor and Legal advice.
Lawyer Liv Sovanna stated, "Currently, the Bar Association has 696 volunteer lawyers to help defend the poor throughout the country in the budget provided by the Royal Government. During the year, the Bar Association assigned lawyers to represent the poor in 5,470 cases, with a total of 8,757 clients (498 women), including 1,721 juvenile cases and minor clients. 2690 people.
He added that in 2023, the Royal Government has provided 2,600 million riels for the defense of the poor to the Bar Association, which can pay for 6,156 cases and 456 legal consultations. In addition, during the year, the Department of Defense for the Poor provided 456 cases of free legal advice under the auspices of the Royal Government.
Lawyer Ly Chantola, President of the Bar Association, said that in addition to the great achievements of the Bar Association in this year, the Bar Association still has some challenges, including:
1. Some rights of lawyers are violated and some work of lawyers is done through intermediaries in some institutions, such as the courts, ministries, departments and some professional institutions.
2. Despite some positive changes, the recognition of the rights of lawyers at the judicial police level in the case of a preliminary investigation into a felony remains a challenge.
3. The implementation of the obligation to pay contributions of some members of the Bar Association is not timely and some do not fulfill the obligation to pay contributions, which shows the carelessness and lack of support for their profession, lack of participation from the Union, such as Expressing opinions on topics that the Bar Association asks all partners to contribute to the common good of the legal profession.
