It also hurts money to be cheated again! 18 Cambodians wait for months for organ transplants in India while nearly $ 1 million was collected by Metro Polyclinic
(Phnom Penh): It was really an accident, I was sick and the money to pay for medical services was collected and escaped. Eighteen Cambodians with kidney problems, commonly known as kidneys, have been waiting for a kidney transplant in India for months, but have not been able to receive treatment due to a fee collected by a representative from the Metro Polyclinic. Escape.
In order to receive kidney transplant treatment in India, a patient from Banteay Meanchey province, who asked to remain anonymous, paid about US $ 50,000 to Nget Sam Oun, director general of Metro LV Polyclinic in Phnom Penh.
The patient went on to say that after receiving the money, he was sent to India for medical treatment. After arriving in India, he did not receive the kidney transplant service as promised by Metro Polyclinic.
He has been waiting for a kidney transplant in India for seven months. According to the patient, he did not receive the kidney transplant because Nget Sam Oun, the director general of the Metro Polyclinic in Phnom Penh, did not pay to go to the Metro Hospital in India. When asked by representatives of the Metro Polyclinic in Phnom Penh, they claimed that the company in Phnom Penh went bankrupt and had no money to pay for the treatment.
In addition to the patients who provided the above information, there are 17 other Cambodian patients who are waiting for a solution and receiving kidney transplant services. But the wait dragged on for months without a solution, and Nget Sam Oun, the recipient of the money, escaped.
According to the above patient, both he and 17 other patients had to have a kidney transplant and had already paid for the transplant. So far, some patients have been waiting for a kidney transplant for 4 months, 5 months, 7 months, 8 months and 9 months.
These 18 Cambodian people with kidney disease and are looking forward to the opportunity to continue their life through kidney transplantation have called for the intervention of Samdech Hun Sen, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, as they have no hope, Mr. Nget Sam Oun, Director General Metro Polyclinic in Phnom Penh will not come forward to solve it.
