Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals, which are held from the 1st of Roach to the 15th of Roach, are the major Khmer Buddhist festivals. Among the Mahayana Buddhist countries, only the Khmer celebrate Kan Ben and Pchum Ben.
Cambodians who believe in Buddhism consider Kanben and Pchum Ben to be a source of food for monks during the rainy season and to support Buddhism. In addition, the Kanben and Pchum Ben festivals are also believed by Buddhists to be an opportunity to dedicate merits to their ancestors and ancestors who have died.
Senior Minister Kun Kim and his family, who have strong faith and devotion to Buddhism as the state religion, are no different from other Buddhists. On the occasion of going to Dak Ben, he brought food, offerings and offerings to the monks, blessed the birth, dedicated to the souls of the ancestors, and then counted the bowls.
In this celebration, Senior Minister Kun Kim also dedicated to the souls of Cambodian warriors who sacrificed in the cause of the motherland during the war to go to their deaths. Also.
