* In Phnom Penh and other provinces, there may be light to moderate rain mixed with thunderstorms with rainfall covering 40-60%. Approve the Pchum Ben festival! Discover the history of Pchum Ben and Kan Ben, which are the traditions of the Khmer people
* History of Pchum Ben
* Reasons for celebrating Pchum
In ancient times, this festival was held to prepare monks for the rainy season in the pagoda for three months from the day of the rainy season to the day of the rainy season. During the rainy season, heavy rains make it difficult for monks to roam. That is why the Buddhists organize this ceremony to supply the monks daily until the rainy day.
On the other hand, in this festival, it is held to spread the devotion to the ghosts, relatives and relatives of the seven clans, as well as many great relatives, through the power of the monks who live and suffer as ghosts. There are karmic prisoners who are severely punished, suffer from hunger, and all of them are free from sorrow and hardship.
* Celebration of Ben Pchum Festival
For this Ben Pchum festival, our Khmer archaeologist has organized for 15 days, starting from the 1st of Roach to the 15th of Roach Khep, as a ceremony to dedicate Kosal to the ghosts of the deceased relatives.
When the drums are marked, it is raining, and the people in each group have to start cooking, and the old people in the group have to choose a house to collect cakes and prepare rice cakes. Prepare and go to the pagoda to listen to the monks chanting the Dharma Sokho and the Dharma Prabhupada.
After that, Mr. Achar, the chairman, had to bring the Buddhists to give (hand over) the food to the monks, turn to Bai Bo, etc. When the time comes for the monks to recite the Keatha at the end of each shift, each group must prepare food to greet the abbot and also arrange a meal for the guests who come to the festival on their day.
