(Prey Veng): On October 11, 2023, the Pearang district administration in Prey Veng province organized a boat race to celebrate the Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals, which are Khmer traditions.
The boat race has 34 boats from different districts in Prey Veng province. The ceremony was also attended by government officials, authorities, the armed forces and more than 7,000 people. The race took place in a happy atmosphere.
Peam Reang District Governor Jem Pra said that the boat race was organized to celebrate Kan Ben and Pchum Ben and to bring happiness to the people. In addition, this boat race is also the ability of the boatmen to participate in the national boat race in November.
Mr. Kong Liva, Assistant to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party, expressed his great pleasure to participate in the traditional Khmer boat race in Pearang district.
Mr. Liva said that the boat race is a ceremony to commemorate the strength of the Khmer Navy in the past. But for now, boating is a demonstration of the unity of the Cambodian people in each boat. This boating provided an educational lesson that unity can only lead to victory.
On that occasion, Mr. Liva reminded the capacity of the boat as well as the participants that the organization of this boat race is possible because Cambodia is peaceful and social stability. On the other hand, if Cambodia experiences the flames of war like in the past or like some countries today, it is unprepared.
In this regard, Mr. Kong Liva called on all people to be united and maintain peace under the leadership of Samdech Prime Minister Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party.
Liva warned people not to believe in any incitement to commit illegal acts that could damage the peace.
Spokesman for the General Commission: Another 387 vehicles were inspected and fined on October 11, 2023
According to the spokesperson, for October 11, 2023, the traffic police nationwide inspected and fined a total of 4,554 vehicles, including 4,103 motorcycles and 651 vehicles.
Notable offenses for motorcycles include: helmet, drunken driving under the influence of drugs, fire / license plate and no license plate. In particular, the car includes: driver's license, speed violation, no lights, check and signal violation.
