Wildlife Alliance Clarifies Allegations That REDD + South Cardamom Route Project Implemented Without Consent Of Indigenous Peoples And Local Communities
The Wildlife Alliance said the participatory land use planning process was carried out in consultation with the full consent of indigenous peoples and local communities in 14 communes.
From 2012 to 2016, the Wildlife Alliance facilitated the survey of 12,248 private plots in compliance with Regulation No. 01 BOP in collaboration with the Ministry of Land Management.
Later, the dam project was abandoned and stopped in 2015, and the new land registration process began in six villages in Chhay Areng.
The Wildlife Alliance has been actively involved in liaising with an organization that is indicting the Red Cardamom South Reds Plus project, providing detailed information in response to their allegations.
Based on the above allegations, Verra, an institution that uses international standards to certify REDD + projects, has stopped issuing carbon certification (VERs) to REDD + projects in the southern Cardamom Mountains until a discovery is made. On quality control in accordance with their preferred conditions. The quality control was completed at the end of July 2023 and left some technical questions to be answered.
Wildlife Alliance believes that the research will show that the Red Cardamom South Rivers Plus project is a responsible and effective conservation initiative that benefits the environment and local communities. It also called for a comprehensive inspection, including the positive impact that REDD + South Cardamom has provided to communities and forests.
In addition, the organization strongly believes that while the REDD + South Cardamom Mountains project is conserving forests and improving the livelihoods of local communities, the project also supports respect for and protection of human rights.
In total, 3,957 families directly benefited from the REDD + South Cardamom mountain range project, including: 94 water wells, 7,800-meter water distribution system, schools, bridges, scholarships and 509 families benefited. Directly in its daily life.
