(Phnom Penh): A well-known agricultural businessman, Duke Hun Lak, encouraged farmers who used to grow vegetables in the field to grow them in their net houses to get high yields and harvest them many times. It supplies the market for many seasons and, in particular, is even more expensive than vegetables grown in the field.
Mr. Sok Sotha, Founder and Executive Director of the Cambodian Farmers Federation Association of Agricultural Producers and Board Member of the Agro-Ecological Network, who trained farmers to grow vegetables in a net house, explained that growing vegetables in a net house is more favorable than growing vegetables. In the field because it yields more and reduces a lot of noise such as pests or climate change.
He said that in general, growing vegetables in a net house, if there is enough water supply, can be grown all year round, unlike growing in the field, can be grown well only in the dry season from November to April, and if When the rainy season comes, it is difficult to plant. The cultivation techniques do not require farmers to know a lot of techniques.
However, he said that what is important is the capital of cultivation in the net house is to invest in the construction of high net houses. In the past, farmers who planted net houses in the size of 8 meters by 25 meters spent about $ 3,000 to build a net house.
"I encourage small farmers to grow vegetables in a net house because they can grow all year round," he said. On the other hand, it can reduce a lot of pests, and care and technicality is better than planting in the field. "But for large farmers, I think it will cost a lot."
He said that in the past, there has been a lot of interest from farmers in growing vegetables in net houses, but they seem reluctant to invest in growing net houses due to the high cost. However, if the relevant partner organizations and the government have financial support for the farmers, they will be able to grow more vegetables in the net house, because for small farmers, it can be a high cost for them.
Regarding the vegetable market in Cambodia, Duke Hun Lak observed that some farmers have now turned to growing vegetables in the net significantly, but he encouraged farmers to grow more vegetables to supply the market and cut. Reduce vegetable imports from neighboring countries.
He said that at present, the inflow of vegetables from abroad is still large, so stakeholders are boosting production to reduce vegetable imports from neighboring countries and help farmers increase productivity and income.
However, he said that the vegetable market in Cambodia seems to be narrow because there is no wholesale market, which is a large market to absorb agricultural products that farmers produce. Therefore, stakeholders need to be more motivated to respond to market production for absorption and competition.
