Please read the following announcement of the provincial administration: The Minister of Information calls on social media users and journalists to stop sharing videos, violence or murder so as not to hurt the families of the victims.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheakdra, Minister of Information, called on all social media users, especially journalists, to stop sharing pictures, videos, violence or murder on both Facebook and TikTok because it hurts the mind and The feelings of the families of the victims who are suffering.
Through Facebook, on October 7, 2023, the Minister of Information claimed that in addition to affecting the emotional well-being of the victims' families, the pictures or videos also affected the mental and emotional well-being of other people. Also.
Minister Neth Pheakdra's call comes after a series of murders in recent days, and images of victims of the atrocities have been widely shared on social media.
Mr. Neth Pheakdra stated, "These days, we have seen the sharing and dissemination of images of violence and murder on Facebook, Tek Tek, telegram and other social networks. Please do not continue to share all kinds of social media users." Share and publish those pictures.
"It has affected the mental and emotional well-being of the families of the victims who are suffering and the loss of their family members, as well as the mental and emotional well-being of other people," he said. Also. "Journalists, please respect the principle of ethics set out in the Law on the Press: Prohibit the publication of pornographic articles and images and images of violence."
To honor the families of the victims and the victims, you can share pictures that do not affect the dignity and privacy of the victims or make the pictures blurred (unclear) before posting and sharing. To do so is to respect both professional ethics and the victims. This is an additional request of the Minister.
