Samleang Seila, Executive Director of APLE, said that the Awareness Raising Program aims to strengthen the knowledge of online child sexual exploitation, reflecting on the Internet use and technology communication of children and youth in Cambodia. Understand the risks of using the Internet and the forms of child sexual exploitation online and how to respond to them.
He also encouraged all participants to actively participate in receiving and sharing knowledge, expressing opinions and continuing to share the knowledge gained to the target groups in the area where the participants are working.
According to research, online sexual abuse and exploitation of children has become a global problem in Cambodia, and it has become a new concern that requires stakeholders, including the public, parents, authorities, civil society, and officials to implement. Laws need to work together to prevent and protect children from this online sexual abuse and exploitation.
The National Policy on Child Protection System (2019-2029) of the Cambodian National Council for Children states that the lack of organization and access to preventive services for children vulnerable to violence, abuse or all forms of exploitation Neglect and exploitation are provided by stakeholders at all levels, which poses a major challenge to the systematic implementation of child protection practices.
According to the APLE report, in the first nine months of 2023, more than 180 cases were reported involving child sexual abuse and exploitation, and 130 cases involved child sexual abuse online.
