Provincial Council and Siem Reap Provincial Authorities Bring Relics to Monks at Wat Prolit Wen Ben 5 to Dedicate Kosal Phal Bon to the Seven Generations
The ceremony was also attended by Mr. To Kim Sean, Ms. Sun Sovanny, Provincial Councilor, Mr. Ung Kim Leang, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap, Mr. Lop Prak Sophoan, Provincial Governor and Mr. Sin Chanthol, Governor of Puok District, together with the ladies and gentlemen of the Provincial Working Group. And many government officials also attended.
Please be reminded that Kan Ben and Pchum Ben are one of the major traditional festivals of Khmer ancestors from ancient times to the present. The Cambodian people always celebrate this festival without interruption until the end of the season, except during the genocidal regime of Democratic Kampuchea Pol Pot, which brought all sectors to zero.
Mr. Ung Kimleang said that during the Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals this year, as well as in previous years, the provincial administration, together with officials and officials in the field of Buddhism, have brought donations and funds to Participate in various programs, never miss.
In fact, this year, despite the situation in a few days in Siem Reap province, some districts suffered severe floods, causing damage to infrastructure, housing, crops, still continue to participate in the Ben in the pagodas. In order to contribute to the supply of food to the monks who remember the rain for a quarter and the monks are studying in the pagoda.
Mr. Ung Kimleang considers Kan Ben and Pchum Ben to be the biggest festivals among other festivals that Cambodian Buddhists always participate in preserving and preserving for the benefit of future generations. .
He continued that, of course, during the Kanben and Pchum Ben festivals, which are the rainy season, thunderstorms often cause difficulties for monks during the Ben Bath cycle, so our Buddhists have established the Kan shift program. This Ben lasts for 15 days during Pchum Ben to make it easier for monks everywhere.
This is due to the right leadership of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which has Samdech Techo Hun Sen, as well as Samdech President Hun Manet, Prime Minister of the 7th legislature, making the country peaceful, stable and prosperous in both Buddhism and the empire.
He added that on behalf of the chairman of the provincial council, the provincial board of governors would like to call on Buddhists in the rainy season to be more vigilant about the recent floods. He also reminded people to continue to take care of their own well-being and replant after the flood to improve their lives.
In addition, the Board of Governors of Siem Reap Province also donated 10 kinds of food and more than 3 million Riels to the monks waiting for the rain in Wat Prolit for the construction of the pagoda and also donated Salad and some suffixes to the monks as well.
Staff of the National Center for Maternal and Child Health donates blood voluntarily to save the lives of those in need
(Phnom Penh): The National Center for Maternal and Child Health, in collaboration with the National Blood Transfusion Center, on October 4, 2023, organized a voluntary and free blood donation ceremony of staff of the National Center for Maternal and Child Health under the theme "Saving lives is donating blood together" under the chairmanship of Dr. Sok Pheng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, representing Professor Chheang Ra, Minister of Health.
On that occasion, Dr. Sok Pheng expressed his great pleasure to participate in the voluntary and free blood donation ceremony in 2023 at the National Center for Maternal and Child Health in collaboration with the National Blood Transfusion Center.
"We need about 300 units of blood a day in Phnom Penh and about 350 units nationwide," he said. He added that this voluntary and free blood donation is very meaningful for three main purposes:
First, to save the lives of our patients, especially patients in Phnom Penh, who need to use more than 80% blood.
In response, Dr. Kim Rathna and staff members are committed to continuing to participate in voluntary and free blood donation to help our people who need blood to survive.
Cambodian Red Cross meets with the Ministry of Health on cooperation within the framework of promoting the promotion of primary health care in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Red Cross on October 4, 2023 met with the Ministry of Health on "Cooperation within the framework of promoting the promotion of primary health care in Cambodia" via video (Zoom).
The meeting was chaired by Ms. Pum Chantyny, Secretary General of the Cambodian Red Cross, Ms. Men Neary Sopheak, First Deputy Secretary General, and Dr. O Vandin, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, as well as relevant officials from the Cambodian Red Cross.
During the meeting, Ms. O Vandin informed about the current situation in promoting primary health care in Cambodia. Good health with the motto "Prevention is better than cure".
She emphasized that the participation of women in each family is a key factor in promoting a healthy family, as the role of women in each family is as a wife and mother, always reminding family members of the importance of Health care, vaccinations, prevention of breastfeeding, infant and child nutrition.
Ms. Pum Chantyny, Secretary General, also stated that the work mentioned by the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health is the work that the Cambodian Red Cross has been doing since 1979, when the Cambodian Red Cross was using its partner's budget. Which applies in the target provinces.
At the same time, Ms. Men Neary Sopheak was also interested in the focus of community health care work, focusing on the participation of individuals, families and communities to promote their health. This is what has been done in line with the Pentagon strategy of the Royal Government of the 7th legislature of the National Assembly, the development of human capital, which aims to make people healthy and capable.
On that occasion, Ms. O Vandin expressed her deep gratitude to the Cambodian Red Cross for always working directly with the community and hoped that the participation of the Cambodian Red Cross will be a good example because the Cambodian Red Cross has all branches. Capitals, provinces, cities, districts and a network of community volunteers.
Please be informed that during the meeting, there was also a presentation on the framework for promoting the promotion of primary health care in Cambodia, highlighting the core activities of empowerment and participation of individuals, families and communities through four main activities by Dr. Chhea Chadaphea, President of the National Center for Health Promotion of the Ministry of Health:
1: Build health literacy and skills of individuals, families and communities to support health, 2: Co-organize for health interventions and primary health care services, 3: Strengthen the structure and participation in health services and accountability to the community 4: Strengthen local leadership for health, including multidisciplinary activities.
The Cambodian Red Cross will strive to fulfill its legal role as an assistant to public authorities in the field of humanitarian causes for the social cause, the future of our country and the future of our children.
At the same time, Ms. O Vandin also expressed her pleasure in waiting for technical assistance anytime, anywhere, any time there is a need from the Cambodian Red Cross to produce fruitful results for Cambodians healthy, happy and long life.
