KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia on Monday announced subsidies and measures to curb rising rice prices amid concerns over rising consumer prices and a shortage of supply. According to Reuters.
Countries across Asia have seen lower supplies and rising rice prices in recent weeks as rising temperatures threaten the harvest and major rice exporters such as India have restricted imports. Out to curb inflation and ensure food security.
Malaysia sets the price of domestic white rice at 2.60 Malaysian ringgit ($ 0.55) per kilogram, Reuters reported.
Malaysia, a country of more than 32 million people, imports about 38 percent of its rice needs and discussed with India last week the lifting of export restrictions.
The Malaysian Ministry of Agriculture and Food Security has said it will subsidize 950 ringgit ($ 201.78) per tonne for white rice imported into Sabah and Sarawak from October 5.
The subsidy will enable imported white rice to have a retail price of 31 ringgit per 10 kilograms in both of Malaysia's most populous states. The ministry also asked the Federal Agricultural Market Authority to increase the distribution of local white rice to rural areas. According to Reuters.
