Floods in Phnom Proek district recede after authorities evacuate more than 100 families to safety
(Battambang): In Phnom Proek district, Battambang province, the flood due to the rain this afternoon has receded after the district authorities evacuated more than 100 families to a safe place to prevent the danger from the flood yesterday.
According to a report from Phnom Proek District Authority, two villages in Phnom Proek District were affected by heavy flooding, including O Village and Phnom Touch Village in Pich Chenda Commune, Phnom Preuk District, Battambang Province on October 2, 2023. This.
The effects of floods and heavy rains since the night of October 2-3, with the attention of the district leadership, led the district command to patrol the area to be careful and intervene to evacuate people. From 131 families, equal to 655 people, take them to a safe place at the nearest high village.
According to local officials, by the afternoon of October 3, the floodwaters had receded slightly, and if the sky stopped raining tonight or tomorrow, the floodwaters would have receded. Sar Sokha urges all stakeholders to work together to combat torture, especially against deprived persons
(Phnom Penh): The Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, Dr. Sar Sokha, urged all stakeholders at the national and sub-national levels to continue to work together to combat torture in all its forms and forms. Especially for those who have been deprived of their liberty.
On the occasion of closing the meeting to review the results of the first mandate and the direction of the National Committee for Combating Torture on the afternoon of October 3, 2023, Mr. Abhisandibandi, Deputy Prime Minister, emphasized that the National Committee must monitor to monitor Regularly inform the commune about the living conditions of the people who have been deprived of their rights and freedoms.
Deputy Prime Minister Sar Sokha added that the National Committee must continue to carry out activities to better educate and revitalize by continuing to strengthen and expand cooperation, mobilization, support from the people, especially other partner organizations to have a better understanding. From the effects of torture on people deprived of their liberty.
However, in order to fight torture in Cambodia more effectively, Dr. Sar Sokha has issued guidelines to all parties, including the National Committee against Torture, the competent authorities and the implementation of duties. Of law enforcement officers as follows:
Main tasks of the National Committee against Torture:
* First, continue to make efforts to educate and disseminate the impact of education in order to raise awareness of the Convention against Torture and its supplementary protocol and other international instruments to stakeholders, especially respect for human rights and the rule of law. Provisions of national and international law
* Second: to monitor regularly the places of detention about the living conditions of people who have been deprived of their rights and freedoms. For example, sanitary buildings should be comfortable, especially for security, which is also a way to strengthen security, prevent and prevent illegal torture. In this case, the relevant authorities responsible for this work, please strengthen and improve legal procedures and, in particular, comply with the provisions in force.
* Third: Building good and close relations with ministries, national institutions, especially sub-national institutions that have the authority to manage communes in order to prevent crimes that are cruel and inhumane.
* Fourth: Continue good relations and cooperation with relevant units, the United Nations, associations, non-governmental organizations and development partners.
* 5: Prepare to investigate suspected cases of torture
* Sixth: Continue to recommend to the relevant authorities and monitor according to the plan, observe and investigate cases of suspected torture, have the authority to commit acts in the territory or may be deprived of their rights and freedoms.
Seventh: Prepare an annual report to the Royal Government, compile a quarterly report and submit it to the Anti-Torture Committee and the United Nations Sub-Committee on Torture Prevention.
* Eighth: Continue to promote better education and be more active by continuing to strengthen and expand cooperation, mobilization, and support from the people, especially other partner organizations, in order to better understand the impact of torture. On people who are deprived of their rights and freedoms
