Evergrande has become a symbol of China's real estate crisis, which has seen many big names get into huge debt, sparking fears of a repercussion for China's wider economy and spread. That could happen all over the world.
"Applications have been submitted by the company to the Stock Exchange for re-trading ... effective from 9 p.m. In the morning of October 3, 2023 onwards.
The real estate giant announced last Thursday that its chairman was suspected of "criminal misconduct" after suspending trading in the company's stock that day.
No specific reason was given for the decision to suspend stock trading last week. The suspension also affected Evergrande's real estate services and its electric car unit.
The incident comes just weeks after the company resumed trading after a 17-month hiatus due to its failure to release its financial results.
HANOI: The Vietnamese government said last weekend that it had launched an investigation that could lead to the imposition of tariffs on anti-dumping duties on wind turbines imported from China. There are lawsuits from local manufacturers.
Reuters reports, quoting a statement issued by the Vietnamese government that manufacturers in Vietnam have claimed that the fraudulent sale of wind turbines of Chinese origin has caused " Significant damage ”to them.
"If necessary, based on the preliminary investigation results, the Ministry of Commerce may take temporary anti-dumping measures to prevent counterfeiting activities affecting domestic production," the statement added.
The Vietnamese government has not set a timeline for the investigation. Neither the Vietnamese Customs nor the Bureau of Statistics provided data on the import of wind power towers.
According to the Vietnamese Ministry of Commerce, local manufacturers have proposed a 97% anti-dumping duty rate.
Wind turbines imported into Vietnam are currently taxed as a 3% preferential country (MFN).
Vietnam is trying to boost wind power as the country begins its transition to carbon neutrality by the middle of the century, aiming to generate 18.5 percent of total wind power by 2030. According to Reuters.
