National Committee Against Torture summarizes the results of the first mandate and raises further directions
(Phnom Penh): The National Committee Against Torture this morning, October 3, 2023, summarized the results of the first mandate and set the direction for further work. The conference was chaired by Mr. Nut Sa An, Chairman of the National Committee Against Torture, and was attended by members of partner organizations and representatives of various relevant ministries and institutions.
The National Committee for Combating Torture, Cruelty, Inhumanity, Persecution, or Punishment (NEC) was established by Royal Decree dated 22 August 2017 to prevent torture, atrocities, and inhumane acts. Acts of persecution or other punishment.
The National Committee Against Torture is a staff of the Royal Government on the prevention and control of torture, atrocities, inhumane acts, persecution, punishment or humiliation. Some of the main tasks of the NEC are:
A good measure to prevent and combat torture is to have a regular inspection mechanism. A total of 270 countries were targeted, with 2,586 stakeholders interviewed, including 1,002 law enforcement officials and 1,584 deprived.
The NEC also investigated and investigated 30 cases of suspected torture, including 11 complaints from the victims' families from 19 media outlets and social media. The case interviewed 110 relevant officials and 106 witnesses. As a result, we have identified 21 cases of malpractice that resulted in serious injuries or even death.
The results of each year are as follows:
* December 2018: Inspected the detention centers in 9 provinces, equal to 18 targets, and interviewed 154 people, including 64 law enforcement officers and 90 people who were deprived of their liberty.
* 2019: Inspected a total of 18 capital-province deprivation sites, equal to 54 targets, and interviewed 486 stakeholders, including 171 law enforcement officers and 315 deprived people. Investigated 9 suspected cases of torture, equivalent to 15 targets, and interviewed 62 people involved, including 32 law enforcement officers and 30 witnesses.
* 2020: Inspected 25 places of deprivation of capital, equal to 77 targets, interviewed 847 people involved, including 331 law enforcement officers and 516 people who were deprived of their liberty. Investigated 3 suspected cases of torture, equal to 8 targets, by interviewing 28 people involved, including 13 law enforcement officers and 15 witnesses.
* 2021: Inspected 19 places of deprivation of capital, equal to 57 targets, and interviewed 456 stakeholders, including 208 law enforcement officers and 248 people who were deprived of their liberty. Investigated 3 suspected cases of torture equal to 8 targets by interviewing 16 people involved, including 12 law enforcement officers and 4 witnesses.
* 2022: Inspected a total of 20 detention centers in 60 provinces, interviewing 611 people, including 216 law enforcement officers and 395 people who were deprived of their liberty. Investigated 8 suspected cases of torture, equal to 31 targets, by interviewing 67 people involved, including 38 law enforcement officers and 29 witnesses.
* 2023 (as of the first half of the year): Inspected the total deprivation of freedom in 1 province, equal to 4 targets, and interviewed 32 stakeholders, including 12 law enforcement officers and 20 deprived people. Figure. Investigated 7 suspected cases of torture, equivalent to 17 targets, by interviewing 58 stakeholders, including 21 law enforcement officers and 37 witnesses.
