(Vientiane): On the morning of October 2, 2023, General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, and his delegation began an official visit to Laos with a warm welcome from his Lao counterpart, Lt. Gen. Kham Leang Ok Thaksin. Deputy Minister of Defense and Chief of Staff of the Lao People's Army.
During the bilateral meeting, the leaders of the two armies discussed the achievements in implementing the cooperation plan between the two armies, including the exchange of visits as well as the cooperation protocol and the organization of the Security Operations Commission meeting. Arranged along the borders of the two countries to respond to activities in a comprehensive and long-term strategic partnership.
In addition, the two armies provided scholarships to each other and exchanged timely intelligence information to jointly crack down on transnational crime, especially drug trafficking, human trafficking, smuggling, arms trafficking and other crimes. Other laws, coordinated through diplomatic channels and all existing mechanisms.
In addition to bilateral cooperation, the two armies also have multilateral cooperation. In fact, in September 2022, the Lao Army hosted a joint disaster relief exercise between Laos, Cambodia and Vietnam in Vientiane. With success.
The two leaders commended the two countries for their commitment to strengthening the friendship, solidarity and cooperation between the two armies, and for their joint commitment to peace, stability, development and upliftment. Livelihood and well-being of the people of both countries as well as in the region.
Senior official of the Ministry of Culture: Chapey Dang Veng Festival is an important cultural event that contributes to the preservation and promotion of this art.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts on October 2, 2023 launched the Chapey Dong Veng National Festival 2023 under the theme "Culture of Peace" under the chairmanship of Mr. Hap Touch, Permanent Secretary of State, on behalf of Dr. Pheung Sakona, Minister Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts.
In addition, the festival also provides an opportunity to learn from each other to strengthen the capacity and quality of the profession of Chapey Dang Veng artists, aiming to preserve the Chapey Dang Veng art form to ensure the survival of the next generation. And can take advantage of this sector to contribute to the development of the national economy and serve the needs of the masses.
This cultural event takes place for 3 days from October 2 to 4, 2023 at the Theater of the Department of Performing Arts, in the presence of the leaders of the civil servants of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, members of the honor program of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia. And many Chapey Dang Veng artists from the capitals and provinces who have applied to compete in their competencies.
On that occasion, Mr. Hap Touch also expressed his appreciation and appreciation for the efforts of the Festival Organizing Committee and the cooperation of the Capital-Provincial Department of Culture and Fine Arts, especially the participation of young Chapey Dang artists. Many long successors of national cultural heritage from the capitals and provinces have made the festival a solemn and proud event. General Knowledge: Lion sculpture in Koh Ker style.
(Phnom Penh): Most of the sculptures in the Koh Ker style are carved from mud, which is rarely seen in bronze, but there is a remarkable bronze lion sculpture that the artist carved on a round pedestal. Connect with the lion at once.
The lion sculpture has round eyes, thick eyebrows, sharp fangs and teeth, curly hair falling in tiers, creating a pattern from the smooth body, and the lion sculpture plays an important role as the guardian of the temple. Of the deity.
The lion symbolizes the devotion associated with the Indian mythology between the goddesses, created by the fiery fire of all the deities to kill the buffalo-headed ascetic named Mahinsak.
They often fall on all kinds of animals, open their mouths, make loud noises, startle animals, and chase away all kinds of things. Both forelegs with sharp nails raised high, a gesture that seemed to suppress something in anticipation of attacking the enemy.
This style reflects the animated animals that represent the Khmer dance and remains an important culture to this day. In Khmer dance, there is a long history before the Angkorian period (6th-8th centuries).
(Phnom Penh): UNICEF Cambodia has pledged to continue its partnership with the Ministry of Rural Development to prevent climate change, rural sanitation and health issues in order to improve the quality and quality of life of rural people. By atmosphere.
He said that in line with the Pentagon strategy - the first phase of the new government, the Ministry of Rural Development has been implementing its vision is to improve the quality and value of life of rural people in a comprehensive environment. Socio-economic and cultural through four priority programs, including:
1. Development of physical infrastructure and rural transport to integrate rural areas into integrated rural areas to promote economic growth and essential basic services to rural areas.
2. Improving the living conditions of rural communities so that people in rural areas can live with dignity, good behavior, good health and good hygiene, and be comfortable.
3. Diversification of rural economic activities through the training of basic skills to create jobs and employment in order to increase household economic income in rural areas.
4. Strengthen administrative and general affairs support services to strengthen national and sub-national institutions to be able to serve rural people with integrity and high responsibility.
The Minister also outlined the work ahead and some of the challenges the Ministry is facing, such as the climate change crisis.
In response, UNICEF Country Director for Cambodia pledged to continue its partnership with the Ministry of Rural Development to prevent climate change, rural sanitation and health issues.
President Will Parks also requested the Ministry of Rural Development to cooperate with relevant ministries and institutions in setting up a rural water supply system for the people.
Minister Chhay Rithy Sen also informed the President of UNICEF that in this seventh mandate, the Ministry has worked closely with the relevant ministries, institutions and units under the umbrella of the Royal Government of Cambodia to provide real benefits to the people.
