(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chea Somethy, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, urged stakeholders to pay more attention to the elderly and encourage local and foreign NGOs to set up care centers for the elderly. There is no need to rely on the provision of care for the elderly through the right professional agencies so that the homeless can get better care.
He said this on the occasion of the 25th anniversary of the Cambodian and International Day of the Elderly on October 2, 2023 at the Ministry of Social Affairs, with more than 200 elderly participants.
"I urge all levels of government, the international community and all stakeholders to pay more attention to the care of the elderly by establishing a management system to ensure "Elderly people are comfortable with our service system for the elderly."
He said that now the common challenge for the elderly, especially those living in remote rural areas, is the lack of access to care and the elderly who are women have to take care of their grandchildren because of their parents. Migration to work or due to divorce or death of parents for various reasons.
He said that in order to solve these problems, the Royal Government, which has the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation and the Cambodian National Committee for the Elderly, as its staff, must continue to strengthen and expand the Association of the Elderly in communes, promoting human development programs. Improve community-based care and family-based elderly care programs, especially the promotion and operation of senior care services to train senior care providers.
According to the Minister of Social Affairs, in 2018, the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation established 1,646 elderly associations in communes and sangkats throughout the provincial capital to alleviate hardship and help each other in case of business.
Mr. Chea Somethy also said that by launching a cash support program for poor families, more than 380,000 elderly people aged 60 and over received cash support from the Royal Government.
At the same time, the Royal Government has increased the policy money for civil servants and the armed forces, especially for retirees, people with disabilities, former civil servants and veterans every year.
According to the 2019 census, the elderly population is close to 1.4 million, accounting for about 8.9 percent of the total population.
