The IMF team ended its visit to Sri Lanka as part of its first review of the country under the bailout agreement, saying "significant progress on key reforms »Is made.
"Discussions will continue toward a short-term staff-level agreement," Bloomberg reported, citing a statement issued by the Washington-based lender. "That will maintain the momentum of reform needed to enable Sri Lanka to recover from its deepening economic crisis."
A source, who asked not to be named, told Reuters that Sri Lanka would meet further with the IMF to ensure it could achieve its goals. The talks could take place in Morocco on the sidelines of next month's IMF annual meeting or in Washington after the meeting, the source added.
The IMF loan program is the driving force behind Sri Lanka's economic recovery after the worst crisis in its history. As part of the bailout budget, the Sri Lankan government needs to reform its state-owned enterprises and improve revenue generation. The country also needs to bring its national debt to a sustainable level after suffering an unprecedented debt default last year.
