Kan Ben Pchum Ben is an important Buddhist festival of all Cambodian Buddhists that is celebrated every year. This festival is held to dedicate merit to the souls of all the meritorious people who have passed away to the next world.
Pchum Ben is one of the largest festivals for Buddhists and lasts for 15 days, from the 1st of Roach to the 15th of Roach Khep. Khmer Buddhists celebrate every year.
According to the records of the history of the Khmer festival, the celebration of this festival to dedicate the merits of the merits to the parents and relatives of the seven children who have passed away so that they can come and receive the merits of the merits to be born in death.
On the other hand, the Cambodian people believe that all the rich people, ancestors or relatives who committed sins while they were alive, and when they passed away, did not know what nationality they were or if they were born as ghosts. Receive this merit from them as well when the ghosts are released for 15 days on the day of Kan Ben.
