Please be informed that as of the announcement of the Ministry of Health on September 29, 2023, Cambodia found 19 cases of Kovid disease: a total of 138,941 people, a total of 135,885 people were cured, a total of 3,056 people died. . Cambodia's Rural Sanitation Program Helps Nearly 350,000 Families in Rural Communities in 6 Provinces "Stop Frequent Defecation"
The four-year project, the Rural Sanitation Program in Cambodia (DIB), strives to stop open defecation and improve the health of rural people in six provinces: Svay Rieng, Kandal, Prey Veng, Kampong Thom. Siem Reap and Oddar Meanchey.
In implementing the Rural Sanitation Program in Cambodia, the Stone Family Foundation is an investor who has pre-funded iDE for the implementation of this program to supply high quality and affordable sanitation facilities to thousands of rural households. Be able to afford that sanitary ware.
From November 2019 to June 2023, the Rural Sanitation Project in Cambodia has successfully assisted nearly 350,000 rural communities to successfully declare their village as “Stop defecation”. Importantly, universal access to sanitation has been highlighted in these rural communities. The Rural Sanitation Program in Cambodia has provided a significant source of funding for iDE to promote a successful sanitation market expansion program.
"Reaching the end of defecation in these communities dates back to the project, which began in 2011 when only a small number of rural households had access to latrines."
Mr. Toch Poliva, Deputy Governor of Svay Rieng Province and Chairman of Svay Rieng Water and Sanitation Working Group, said that the trip to the province, which declared the first open defecation in Svay Rieng, was an example of cooperation. Commitment, sharing of responsibilities between public institutions and development partners. Prioritizing access to clean water and better sanitation.
"We have not only achieved national goals, we have also changed the mindset of rural communities," he added. Collaboration at all levels has led to three major changes in hygiene behaviors: regular use of toilets, regular drinking of water and regular hand washing with soap. "This success will be an example of the change that comes from sharing responsibility to ensure well-being in our society."
