NEC Chairman Calls on Citizens to Review 2023 Voter List and Voter Registration (Video inside) (Phnom Penh) Voting names for 2023 start from October 1 to 19, 2023 in commune halls and in some villages in communes across the country.
It should be informed that the 2023 voter list and voter registration review aims to ensure that the voter list is complete, accurate and secure by:
* Extracting and deleting the names of voters: - who have died - who have been temporarily deprived of the right to vote - who have moved or relocated to another commune with valid and clear documents to be removed from the voter list
Correction of names or voter data at the request of the applicant based on valid credentials. * Voter registration refers to:
Khmer citizens who have the right to vote and have not yet registered to vote before - Khmer citizens who are 18 years old or older (born before or born on January 1, 2006) - Khmer people who have moved Or a place to stay in a new commune / sangkat must go to register to vote in the commune / sangkat where the person concerned is living or residing.
The National Election Committee (NEC) strongly hopes that all Cambodians with sufficient conditions and documents will understand the importance of voter registration and voter registration, and visit the voter list and voter registration. Each and on time, do not interrupt.
Three Ministers Inspect Water Condition, Infrastructure and Crops in Vaiko Irrigation System, Prey Veng Province
(Prey Veng): Mr. Chhay Rithy Sen, Minister of Rural Development, Mr. Thor Chetha, Minister of Water Resources and Meteorology and Mr. Dith Tina, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, in the morning of September 29, 2023, inspected the water condition of infrastructure and Cultivation work at Vaiko Irrigation System, Prey Veng Province.
This irrigation development project takes water from the Mekong River, which has a catchment area of 795,000 square meters, an average annual flow of 14,500 cubic meters per second.
The first phase of the station is responsible for diverting water from the Mekong River by connecting the upper reaches of the small rivers and canals to irrigate agricultural land in Prey Veng, Svay Rieng and Kampong Cham provinces.
Delegates from the three ministries inspected the second phase of the irrigation system, located in Prey Veng province, which has the potential to irrigate 93,100 hectares in the rainy season and 23,300 hectares in the dry season.
