(Phnom Penh): 2023 is the 70th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations between Japan and Cambodia, which began in 1953. In 1954, one year after the establishment of diplomatic relations, Cambodia relinquished its right to claim World War II compensation from Japan.
The Japanese government paid a courtesy call on King Norodom Sihanouk to visit Japan in 1955. While on a royal visit, the National Assembly of Japan approved a vote of thanks to Cambodia.
* First: Cooperation in diplomatic relations between Cambodia and Japan
Cambodia-Japan has been cooperating since the 17th century, if you look at it, there are many stories of exchanges and relations. Official relations between the two countries were formally established on January 9, 1953, 10 months before Cambodia declared its independence.
Japan drafted the Cambodian Peace Treaty in 1990, a turning point in the signing of the 1991 peace treaty, at which time there was the United Nations Interim Authority (UNTAC) in Cambodia. Japan also sent peacekeepers to join the peacekeeping mission in Cambodia.
After Cambodia achieved full peace, Japan contributed to the development of Cambodian human resources by sending people to study in Japan after many Cambodian intellectuals lost their lives during the Pol Pot genocidal regime. In addition, Japan has continued to cooperate with Cambodia in demining, conservation and restoration of some temple sites in the Angkor Wat resort area.
* Second: Current bilateral relations
The Prime Minister of Cambodia Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia for the 7th mandate, on the occasion of attending the ASEAN Summit and related meetings in Jakarta, Republic of Indonesia on September 7, 2023, also met with the Prime Minister of Japan on cooperation. In many areas, both regional affairs and the future of Cambodia, as well as comprehensive strategic partnerships.
Through this meeting, the Prime Ministers of Cambodia and Japan agreed to further strengthen and expand the cooperation between the two countries to be even closer through partnership in support of responding to regional and global situations. Through 70 years of diplomatic relations, Japan has supported Cambodia in socio-economic work by providing official development assistance (RDA) as well as the activities of a number of Japanese NGOs.
Cambodia has supported Japan's position on the political landscape, and more recently, as the first ASEAN member, has expressed support for the Asia-Pacific Open Agreement. Recently, the Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia announced restrictions on the import of fishery products from Japan to Cambodia after the release of nuclear power from the plant in Fukushima, which is strongly applauded by the Japanese government to Cambodia.
The current Cambodian-Japanese cooperation has focused on security issues, with two major exchanges between senior officials from the Japanese Self-Defense Forces and Cambodian forces at the port of Sihanoukville in 2022. In 2023, the two sides also organized cooperation between the two forces.
* Third: What is the future concept of diplomatic relations between Cambodia and Japan?
The Cambodia-Japan Comprehensive Strategic Partnership is not just about Cambodia-Japan relations, but the two countries working together as counterparts in responding to regional and global challenges together.
Through a comprehensive strategic partnership, Cambodia and Japan should further enhance cooperation for regional and international prosperity.
This relationship is symbolic through the joint efforts of Cambodia and Japan, which have noted the training of Ukrainian deminers. The initiative has been praised and supported by the international community, and Cambodia has not only helped train Ukrainian deminers, but also Iraqi, Colombian and other countries through JICA.
He believes that there is still much work that Cambodia and Japan can do together to build peace, which is not only beneficial for the region, but for the whole world.
In a statement, the Cuban Foreign Ministry said no one was injured in the attack, and at the request of the Cuban embassy, officials from the US Secret Service arrived. Scene to verify the attack. The ministry also warned that loopholes for US law enforcement officials could encourage such attacks in the future.
According to the Cuban Foreign Ministry, this is the second attack on the Cuban embassy in Washington since April 2020. At the time, a Cuban-American citizen fired a shotgun at the Cuban embassy. Fortunately, no Cuban officials were injured in the incident, with only minor damage.
For more than three years, the perpetrators have been waiting for their trial, and the US government does not consider this an act of terrorism. The Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations requires the United States to take the necessary steps to protect the embassy building against any aggression or damage and to prevent any act that could disrupt the peace or dignity of foreign embassies on the territory. Its.
The Cuban Foreign Ministry condemned the terrorist act and hoped that the US government would act in accordance with the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations to prevent such acts from recurring.
