Two decades after the invasion, the victims of torture in Iraq are still waiting for compensation from the United States
LONDON (Reuters) - The United States government has failed to provide compensation to Iraqis who have been brutally tortured for two decades after evidence emerged that US forces had tortured prisoners in the country's prisons. Iraq. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on September 25, 2023.
Human Rights Watch and other organizations have documented torture and other abuses by US forces in Iraq. Survivors of the abuse have sought justice, while the US government has received little recognition or compensation.
"Twenty years later, Iraqis tortured by US officials still have no clear way to sue or receive compensation or recognition," said Sarah Yaeger, director of Human Rights Watch in Washington. "Not from the US government."
"US officials want to get rid of torture in the past, but the long-term effects of torture remain a daily reality for many Iraqis and their families," she added. Photo from AFP Nuon Pharath highlights the president's priority policy program on the sidelines of the World Summit in South Korea
